src/numeric.ts (developer-b, published reliability helper):
export type ParseOperandResult = { readonly ok: true; readonly value: number; readonly normalized: string } | { readonly ok: false; readonly error: string };
export function parseOperand(raw: string): ParseOperandResult;
export function formatSum(value: number): string;
Contract: `parseOperand` first trims `raw`; if the trimmed value is empty it returns `{ ok: false, error: 'operand must not be empty' }`; it accepts only trimmed strings matching `/^[+-]?(?:\d+(?:\.\d*)?|\.\d+)$/`; it converts with `Number`, requires `Number.isFinite`, and returns `{ ok: true, value, normalized: String(value) }`. Thus `" 1.0 "` is accepted and normalized to `"1"`; `""`, `"   "`, `"abc"`, `"NaN"`, and `"Infinity"` are rejected. `formatSum(value)` returns `String(value)` and is used for the exact stdout numeric form.

src/index.ts (developer-a, entry point):
export function main(argv: readonly string[]): number;
Import path: `import { parseOperand, formatSum } from './numeric.js';`
Observable CLI contract: `main(['sum','1','2'])` writes exactly `3\n` to stdout and returns `0`; `main(['sum','-2.5','0.5'])` writes exactly `-2\n` and returns `0`; `main(['sum','1.0','1'])` writes exactly `2\n` and returns `0`. If argv has no command, an unknown command, or not exactly two operands after `sum`, write `Error: usage: sum <a> <b>\n` to stderr, write nothing to stdout, and return `1`. If either operand fails `parseOperand`, write `Error: operands must be finite decimal numbers; usage: sum <a> <b>\n` to stderr, write nothing to stdout, and return `1`. On success write `${formatSum(a + b)}\n` to stdout only. When executed as `src/index.ts`, set `process.exitCode = main(process.argv.slice(2))`.
